In release builds
-----------------

PostgreSQL and pgvector
  The desktop app bundles a managed PostgreSQL runtime (with pg_trgm) and
  the pgvector extension, at the versions pinned in
  packages/db/runtime/postgres/version.ts. Both are under the PostgreSQL
  License. Their license texts ship in the pg directory of the app
  resources: pgvector-LICENSE on every platform, plus server_license.txt
  and commandlinetools_3rd_party_licenses.txt on Windows and
  postgresql-COPYRIGHT on macOS and Linux. On Windows the PostgreSQL
  binaries come from the EDB binary archive, which bundles other libraries;
  their licenses are in commandlinetools_3rd_party_licenses.txt.
  Sources: https://www.postgresql.org and https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector

ripgrep
  Bundled search binary, with its LICENSE-MIT in the rg directory of the
  app resources.
  Source: https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep

Node.js
  Bundled runtime for the Snaglet server sidecar, at the version pinned in
  apps/desktop/scripts/stage-sidecar.mjs. Its license ships as
  server/node-LICENSE in the app resources.
  Source and license: https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.22.0/LICENSE

Microsoft Visual C++ runtime (Windows builds only)
  The redistributable runtime DLLs from the build machine's Visual Studio
  installation, under Microsoft's redistribution terms.

npm packages and Rust crates
  The server bundle and the web UI include npm packages listed in
  pnpm-lock.yaml, and the desktop shell is compiled from the Rust crates in
  apps/desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.lock. Each keeps the license declared in its
  package metadata.

Models that Snaglet downloads on first use (embedding, speech) are not part of
this repository or the app bundle and carry their own licenses. So does the
optional reranking model, which is off by default and downloaded only after
its feature flag is turned on.

